  "answer": "**Not yet.** Inflation has picked up again since mid-2025 and is running above the RBA's 2–3% target band, so the RBA has been tightening.\n\n- **Headline CPI:** 4.0% year-on-year in August 2026 (latest monthly figure). It peaked at 4.6% in March 2026 and eased to 3.5% in July before rising again. In April 2025 it was 2.4%.\n- **Trimmed mean (the RBA's preferred underlying measure):** 3.6% in August 2026, up from 3.2% in April 2025. It has been above 3% since September 2025 and has risen for most of 2026. That is the more worrying signal because it strips out volatile items, so the increase is broad-based rather than a few spikes.\n- **Policy response:** The cash rate was 3.60% from September 2025 to January 2026. The RBA then raised it to 4.35% by June 2026, and the September 2026 monthly average was 4.36%.\n\nInflation is well below its 2023 peak (trimmed mean 6.5% in Q1 2023), so it is not out of control in that sense. But on the latest data it is still above target and rising, and the RBA is responding. The September 2026 CPI, due soon, will show whether the trend has turned.",
